file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<file name=\"/home/yarin.b/projects/excel_magic/artifacts/runs/20261003T091508Z_J17_pi_qwen3coder_kumoL_rec_airfoil_self_noise/search/airfoil_self_noise/workspace/TASK.md\">\nYou are the TabFM-Auto pipeline-search agent. A FROZEN tabular foundation model is fixed; you only\nedit the data pipeline around it (pipeline.py) to minimise the 3-fold cross-validation error reported by\n`tabfm-eval`. Rules:\n- Only edit pipeline.py. Only run `tabfm-eval` (and read-only shell commands / small python snippets to inspect\n  train.parquet). Never try to read, list or guess anything outside this directory. Never use the network.\n- Never replace the frozen model, train another model inside pipeline.py, or hard-code label information.\n- Keep every improvement that lowers the CV score; revert edits that make it worse (candidates/ keeps snapshots).\n- You may use pandas, numpy, scipy and scikit-learn (transforms only) inside pipeline.py.\n- Think about the column names and task description: domain formulas, ratios, log transforms of skewed targets,\n  missing-value sentinels, entity co-occurrence counts for ID columns, class-balanced context views, prior /\n  temperature calibration in postprocess().\n- When the evaluation budget is exhausted or you cannot improve further, stop. The harness picks the best\n  candidate automatically; leave pipeline.py equal to your best candidate.\n\n# Task\nDataset: airfoil_self_noise  |  type: regression  |  target column: `scaled-sound-pressure`\nMetric: rmse (lower is better), 3-fold CV on train.parquet (801 rows, 5 columns).\nFrozen model: kumo-tabular-l:n_estimators=8,device=cuda (TabPFN v2, in-context learner; context limited to max_rows=10000).\nBudget: 24 evaluations / 60 minutes. Identity pipeline P0 scored 1.42782 (rmse, lower is better) with 5 features.\n\n## Dataset description\nTabArena dataset airfoil_self_noise (OpenML task 363612). This dataset was curated for [TabArena](https://tabarena.ai/) by the TabArena team\nas part of the [TabArena Tabular ML IID Study](https://tabarena.ai/data-tabular-ml-iid-study).\nFor more details on the study, see our [paper](https://tabarena.ai/paper-tabular-ml-iid-study).\n\n**Dataset Focus**: This dataset shall be used for evaluating predictive machine\nlearning models for independent and identically distributed tabular data. The\nintended task is regression.\n\n---\n#### Dataset Metadata\n- **Licence:** CC BY 4.0\n- **Original Data Source:** https://doi.org/10.24432/C5VW2C\n- **Reference (please cite)**: Brooks, Thomas F., D. Stuart Pope, and Michael A. Marcolini. Airfoil self-noise and prediction. No. L-16528. 1989. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19890016302\n- **Dataset Year:** 2014\n- **Dataset Description:** see the reference and the original data source for details.\n\n#### Curation comments by the TabArena team (for code see the [page of the study](https://tabarena.ai/data-tabular-ml-iid-study)):\n- N/A\n\n## Columns\n  - frequency\n  - attack-angle\n  - chord-length\n  - free-stream-velocity\n  - suction-side-displacement-thickness\n\n\n\n# How to work\n1. `cat pipeline.py` to see the current (identity) pipeline and the contract of the four hooks.\n2. Inspect the data briefly (`python -c \"import pandas as pd; df=pd.read_parquet('train.parquet'); print(df.describe(include='all').T)\"`).\n3. Edit pipeline.py, then run `tabfm-eval` and read the score. Repeat.\n4. Finish with pipeline.py equal to your best candidate and write 3-6 lines in NOTES.md explaining what helped.\n\n</file>\n"}],"timestamp":1791018933036},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll analyze the task and work on improving the pipeline for the airfoil_self_noise dataset. Let me start by examining the current pipeline and the data.\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8a91e5b00bf1450bb6601b41","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"pipeline.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":2590,"output":52,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":2642,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791018933077,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9beb4880043863ac","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8a91e5b00bf1450bb6601b41","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"\"\"Candidate data pipeline around a FROZEN tabular foundation model.\n\nThe harness calls, in order:\n  1. preprocess(X_train, y_train, X_test)        -> X_train, y_train, X_test   (cleaning, target transform)\n  2. engineer(X_train, y_train, X_test)          -> X_train, X_test            (feature engineering, <= 500 cols)\n  3. sample(X_train, y_train, X_test, max_rows)  -> list of index arrays       (context views; each view is fit separately\n                                                                                 and predictions are averaged)\n  4. frozen model fit/predict per view (never edit this part; MODEL_KWARGS tunes the constructor)\n  5. postprocess(pred, y_train_orig, X_test)     -> pred                       (invert target transforms, calibrate)\n\n`pred` is a pandas DataFrame of class probabilities (columns = class labels) for classification, or a\npandas Series for regression. `y_train_orig` is the untransformed training target. All inputs are pandas\nobjects; X_train / X_test share columns. Do not read any file other than the ones in this directory.\n\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nMODEL_KWARGS = {}  # constructor settings for the frozen model; {} keeps the defaults\n\n\ndef preprocess(X_train, y_train, X_test):\n    return X_train, y_train, X_test\n\n\ndef engineer(X_train, y_train, X_test):\n    return X_train, X_test\n\n\ndef sample(X_train, y_train, X_test, max_rows):\n    return [np.arange(len(X_train))]  # one context view with all training rows\n\n\ndef postprocess(pred, y_train_orig, X_test):\n    return pred\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791018933530},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let me examine the data to understand its structure and characteristics.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_da122f25afc54f76848e6091","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python -c \"import pandas as pd; df=pd.read_parquet('train.parquet'); print(df.describe(include='all').T)\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":3024,"output":61,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":3085,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791018933534,"responseId":"chatcmpl-89b78526a73f9518","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_da122f25afc54f76848e6091","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"                                     count unique  ...       75%       max\nfrequency                            801.0    NaN  ...    4000.0   20000.0\nattack-angle                           801     27  ...       NaN       NaN\nchord-length                         801.0    NaN  ...    0.2286    0.3048\nfree-stream-velocity                 801.0    NaN  ...      71.3      71.3\nsuction-side-displacement-thickness  801.0    NaN  ...  0.016104  0.058411\nscaled-sound-pressure                801.0    NaN  ...   129.949   140.987\n\n[6 rows x 11 columns]\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791018935162},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let me also check the column names and get a quick look at the data:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_20a418088c2a4fdca258617f","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python -c \"import pandas as pd; df=pd.read_parquet('train.parquet'); print(df.columns.tolist()); print(df.head())\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":3300,"output":65,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":3365,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791018935165,"responseId":"chatcmpl-843fdd0f5f315a1b","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_20a418088c2a4fdca258617f","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"['frequency', 'attack-angle', 'chord-length', 'free-stream-velocity', 'suction-side-displacement-thickness', 'scaled-sound-pressure']\n   frequency  ... scaled-sound-pressure\n0       8000  ...               115.372\n1       4000  ...               133.223\n2       3150  ...               113.055\n3       1600  ...               133.241\n4        800  ...               123.227\n\n[5 rows x 6 columns]\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791018936468},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I'll run the baseline evaluation to confirm the initial score:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_38db0171f7924767be53d70c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"tabfm-eval"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":3522,"output":38,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":3560,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791018936471,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b39427796a18e3e5","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_38db0171f7924767be53d70c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"eval #2: rmse = 1.42782 (+/- 0.1480 over 3 folds) \n  features=5 views=1 time=11.19s | P0=1.42782 best_before=1.42782\n  secondary: rmse=1.4278, mae=0.8956, r2=0.9563\n  budget: 2/24 evaluations used\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791018949319},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good, I've confirmed the baseline score of 1.42782. Now I'll think about improvements for the pipeline. Looking at the dataset, I notice several potential improvements:\n\n1. The target variable is `scaled-sound-pressure` which might benefit from log transformation if it's skewed\n2. There could be useful feature engineering combinations\n3. Some features might benefit from scaling or transformations\n\nLet me create an improved pipeline by adding feature engineering and potentially transforming the target:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3c58828eda3c4b0fb0363f68","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"pipeline.py","edits":"[{\"oldText\": \"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nMODEL_KWARGS = {}  # constructor settings for the frozen model; {} keeps the defaults\n\n\ndef preprocess(X_train, y_train, X_test):\n    return X_train, y_train, X_test\n\n\ndef engineer(X_train, y_train, X_test):\n    return X_train, X_test\n\n\ndef sample(X_train, y_train, X_test, max_rows):\n    return [np.arange(len(X_train))]  # one context view with all training rows\n\n\ndef postprocess(pred, y_train_orig, X_test):\n    return pred\", \"newText\": \"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nMODEL_KWARGS = {}  # constructor settings for the frozen model; {} keeps the defaults\n\n\ndef preprocess(X_train, y_train, X_test):\n    # Try log transformation on target if it's skewed\n    # Check skewness of the target\n    y_train_log = np.log(y_train + 1e-8)  # Add small epsilon to avoid log(0)\n    \n    # If the log-transformed target has lower skewness, use it\n    original_skew = pd.Series(y_train).skew()\n    log_skew = pd.Series(y_train_log).skew()\n    \n    if abs(log_skew) < abs(original_skew):\n        y_train = y_train_log\n    \n    return X_train, y_train, X_test\n\n\ndef engineer(X_train, y_train, X_test):\n    # Create engineered features that might be relevant for airfoil noise prediction\n    # Based on physical relationships in aerodynamics\n    \n    # Ratio features that might matter\n    X_train_engineered = X_train.copy()\n    X_test_engineered = X_test.copy()\n    \n    # Create some physically meaningful combinations\n    X_train_engineered['frequency_chord_ratio'] = X_train['frequency'] / (X_train['chord-length'] + 1e-8)\n    X_test_engineered['frequency_chord_ratio'] = X_test['frequency'] / (X_test['chord-length'] + 1e-8)\n    \n    X_train_engineered['velocity_frequency_ratio'] = X_train['free-stream-velocity'] / (X_train['frequency'] + 1e-8)\n    X_test_engineered['velocity_frequency_ratio'] = X_test['free-stream-velocity'] / (X_test['frequency'] + 1e-8)\n    \n    # Product features\n    X_train_engineered['frequency_velocity_product'] = X_train['frequency'] * X_train['free-stream-velocity']\n    X_test_engineered['frequency_velocity_product'] = X_test['frequency'] * X_test['free-stream-velocity']\n    \n    # Dimensionless groups (like Reynolds number components)\n    X_train_engineered['reynolds_like'] = X_train['frequency'] * X_train['chord-length'] / (X_train['free-stream-velocity'] + 1e-8)\n    X_test_engineered['reynolds_like'] = X_test['frequency'] * X_test['chord-length'] / (X_test['free-stream-velocity'] + 1e-8)\n    \n    # Scaled features for better numerical stability\n    scaler = StandardScaler()\n    numeric_cols = ['frequency', 'attack-angle', 'chord-length', 'free-stream-velocity', 'suction-side-displacement-thickness']\n    \n    X_train_scaled = X_train_engineered[numeric_cols].copy()\n    X_test_scaled = X_test_engineered[numeric_cols].copy()\n    \n    X_train_scaled = pd.DataFrame(scaler.fit_transform(X_train_scaled), columns=numeric_cols)\n    X_test_scaled = pd.DataFrame(scaler.transform(X_test_scaled), columns=numeric_cols)\n    \n    # Combine scaled features with engineered ones\n    for col in numeric_cols:\n        X_train_engineered[col] = X_train_scaled[col]\n        X_test_engineered[col] = X_test_scaled[col]\n    \n    return X_train_engineered, X_test_engineered\n